WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.110
Phillip: What are the differences between
an ineffective team and an effective team?

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:09.150
Ineffective teams are usually made up of
people who don’t really work well together.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:13.440
Maddy: Individuals within the team
may show favouritism, put their own

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:17.740
goals ahead of team goals, and deflect
responsibility for their own mistakes.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:21.729
Some members may not even have
the appropriate skills or too many

00:00:21.729 --> 00:00:23.640
members may have the same skills.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:28.600
Ineffective teams may also take actions
before issues are examined or resolved,

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.920
leading to unconstructive disagreements
and members feeling vulnerable.

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:36.989
Phillip: Effective teams on the other
hand are made up of people who have

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:41.039
complimentary skills and can work together
to accomplish the goals of the team.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.889
So, let’s have a look at behaviours that might
be seen in effective teams in more detail.

00:00:47.339 --> 00:00:48.349
Maddy: Safe to be vulnerable

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:53.199
In an effective team, no one feels
like they need to keep their guard up.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:58.360
That’s because they know that everyone is on
the same side, working towards the same goals.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:03.529
They know their team will support them,
encourage them, and help them grow and achieve.

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:08.020
They also know they’re safe to express
ideas without the fear of ridicule.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:13.899
For example, a new team member proposes an
idea around tackling a difficult part of the

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:19.729
current project, but they don’t realise this
has been tried before and found not to work.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:25.069
The team won’t criticise or mock them
for suggesting it, instead it’s used

00:01:25.069 --> 00:01:29.119
as a learning opportunity to explain
why it didn’t work, and time is taken

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.659
to thank them for their contribution.

00:01:31.669 --> 00:01:32.819
Phillip: Ego-free zone

00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:37.860
In an effective team, people aren’t out
for themselves, they’re there for the team.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:44.100
Although individual ambition should be encouraged,
in an effective team a person’s ambitions

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.339
are caught up in the success of their team.

00:01:47.019 --> 00:01:51.950
They put their team first and invest their energy
and talent into achieving the team’s goals.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:53.990
Maddy: Constructive disagreements

00:01:54.929 --> 00:01:59.669
Team members will always have disagreements,
but in an effective team, these should

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.699
be about finding the right solution, and
not about people just pushing their own

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.720
agenda or opinion, for the sake of it.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.350
Disagreements and debates are
respectful and polite, and personal

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:11.930
feelings are kept to one side.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.519
The target is always about finding the best
solution to allow the team to hit their goals.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.299
For example, during a tense part of a project,
there are different possible pathways through.

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:28.650
During a discussion about this, two team
members in particular, have differing, but

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.000
very passionate ideas about what will work.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:36.230
Although they’re forthright with one another,
they still give each other time to fully explain

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:38.990
their proposed idea so the team can decide.

00:02:39.519 --> 00:02:44.030
And, when an option is selected, the
person whose idea wasn’t used is thanked

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.300
for their contribution to the discussion,
and they respect the team’s decision.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:50.170
Phillip: Accountability

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.120
In an effective team, people take
responsibility for their actions.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.480
They don’t try and deflect or blame
others if things go wrong, instead

00:02:59.669 --> 00:03:01.470
they acknowledge their mistakes openly.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.400
This is why ‘safe to be vulnerable’ is important.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:11.440
When a mistake happens, it’s safe to take
ownership of it because everyone’s main concern

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.210
is making things better, not handing out blame.

00:03:14.809 --> 00:03:20.170
Accountability also allows people to feel pride
in their work when things go right, as long

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.200
as they recognise the role of the team too.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:23.480
Maddy: Trust

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:26.660
Members of an effective team trust one another.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:31.660
They know that everyone is there for the same
reason and that everyone wants to work together.

00:03:32.279 --> 00:03:36.129
By showing one another trust,
the team will show you trust too.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:38.810
Phillip: Honesty and integrity

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:43.470
An effective team is one where people
hold themselves to the same standards

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:44.690
that they hold their peers to.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.859
They’re honest about themselves, what they’re
worried about and what they’re proud of.

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:52.999
They don’t apply double standards or favouritism.

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:57.519
They respect everyone’s achievements
and help everyone through their trials.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:03.970
So, you can see effective teams create bonds
and strong relationships between members, and

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.299
understand the goals and targets expected of them.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:11.019
They work as a team, for the team
to achieve their defined team goals,

00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:13.420
and not as individuals with agendas.

